  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">OEGI - Western Kenya Sanitation Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Kenya):  Using FY 2022 funds, USAID will continue its support for the Western Kenya Sanitation Activity that is working to develop a robust sanitation and hygiene market to enable the adoption of higher quality latrines and county-wide access to hygiene products. The activity develops and reinforces market-based solutions, partnerships and learning interventions which address systemic barriers to improved sanitation, hygiene and menstrual health hygiene.The county-wide approach of strengthening markets for sanitation and hygiene products and services will address the need for higher quality household sanitation facilities in rural areas; developing new initiatives beyond the traditional &quot;Do-It-Yourself&quot; model promoted through Community Led Total Sanitation. The activity will design and implement services, solutions and products that address &quot;user needs&quot; and preferences within their environments. Prior to approval, the activity will solicit customers&apos; insights and reviews for prototypes while paying special attention to usability, practicality, and pricing.  The activity will engage new and non-traditional partners to foster stable, resilient, and more self-reliant counties. Interventions will include capacity building of county governments and other stakeholders on the environmental impacts of poor community level sanitation and waste management. The Western Kenya Sanitation Activity will support the objectives of the 2022-2027 USAID Biodiversity Policy and Climate Strategy and contributes to the U.S. Global Water Strategy Strategic Objective One: Increase sustainable access and use of sanitation and the practice of key hygiene behaviors. Sustainable access to water and sanitation helps reduce conflict over resources, contributing to greater regional stability, a U.S. Foreign Policy priority. $1,200,000 will be used to meet the &quot;safe latrines&quot; earmark under this activity.. .</narrative>
  </description>
